cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  tscausal: time-symmetric process matrices without selection — what time
  symmetry forbids and what it does not
message: "If you use these materials, please cite the Zenodo record."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Artem
    family-names: Oktyabrev
    email: aoktyabrev@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-3626-2002"
abstract: >-
  A computational study of time-symmetric process matrices (Mrini-Hardy
  formalism, arXiv:2406.18489) in the regime without pre- or postselection.
  Results: without selection every bipartite process reduces to a mixture of
  one-way channels (Lemma D6), so the facets N1 and N2 witness nothing about
  causal indefiniteness; a definite-order process without selection is exactly a
  Bell scenario, and the quantum-classical gap on the classes cl3-cl6 is CHSH
  and the Tsirelson bound in relabelled roles (Lemma D8); an explicit classical,
  causally non-separable three-party process without selection is constructed,
  violating published causal inequalities. The real-quantum-theory loophole of
  Hoffreumon-Woods (arXiv:2603.19208) is shown not to transfer into the class
  ISO: at its own operations, the maximum of Renou'"'"'s functional over all real
  ISO processes is certified to be at most 3*sqrt(2), exactly half of the
  complex value. Each stage is sealed by a preregistration of predictions before
  its first run; RESULTS.md is generated by a script.
keywords:
  - process matrices
  - indefinite causal order
  - time symmetry
  - real quantum theory
  - causal inequalities
  - semidefinite programming
  - preregistration
